# **`lose-what-is-not-kept` is `spec/turn.md`'s third step, in its own words**: *what expires
# expires, and what was not kept in order is lost*. **It comes before the refreshes**, which is the
# order that document states - upkeep, then growth, then this, then what nature reclaims, and time
# restoring the counts last.
#
# **Its one clause has the role `keep`**, which bounds a quantity to the room there is for it. That
# is the fifth role and the first that reads a capacity: `require` and `remove` match, `add` makes,
# `put` assigns, and this one takes away what will not fit.

# **It takes no argument, and the sweep is what said so.** It had a `what`, and
# `{relation-of clause:clause-20 input:what}` to read it - but the clause already declares
# `relation:resource`, and `resource` is the only loose family, so the argument could only ever be
# what the clause already said. **Deleting the `relation-of` row broke nothing**, which is the
# sweep reporting decoration rather than a rule.
#
# **So the input, the `relation-of` and the argument are all gone**, and the rule says what it does.
# If a second family is ever loose, the argument comes back - and then it will be doing something.

{rule id:7 name:lose-what-is-not-kept}

{clause id:20 rule:7 seq:1 role:5 relation:27}

# **`upkeep` is `spec/turn.md`'s first step**: *upkeep is paid*. One citizen eats one food and is
# no longer hungry, and it repeats - so as many eat as there is food for, and nothing anywhere
# computes a minimum. Sean, 2026-09-19, on the loop he intends: *each citizen cosumes 1 food or
# perishes*.
#
# **It is scoped per territory**, which is what joins the citizen to the food. Without it the
# clause would name no place, match a citizen in every territory at once, and be refused for not
# having said which - `Refused::NotOneToTake`.
#
# **Both clauses name every trait they mean.** `hunger:1` is what makes one of the two citizen rows
# in a place the one meant, and it is why nothing here has to choose.

{rule id:8 name:upkeep}

{input id:15 rule:8 seq:1 name:where of:13}

{scope rule:8 input:15}
{repeats rule:8}

{clause id:21 rule:8 seq:1 role:2 relation:47}
{clause id:22 rule:8 seq:2 role:2 relation:31}
{clause id:23 rule:8 seq:3 role:3 relation:47}

